Not exactly — that order is okay for a very small toy app, but for this school system, the better order is:

1. Structure first
- Decide pages and components
- Login
- Dashboard
- Class register
- Score entry
- Master sheet
- Reports
- Permissions

2. HTML first
- Build the page structure
- Make each screen skeleton
- Not full logic yet

3. CSS second
- Match the design style from the reference
- Sidebar, cards, tables, buttons, colors, spacing

4. JavaScript third
- Add interactions
- Dropdowns
- Filters
- Search
- Modals
- Form validation
- Table updates

5. Database model next
- Students
- Teachers
- Classes
- Subjects
- Terms
- Results
- Permissions
- Audit logs

6. Supabase or backend next
- Real data storage
- Role-based access
- Result saving
- Score calculations

7. Authentication after data structure
- Login
- Session management
- Protected pages
- Role checks

8. Then business logic
- Grades
- Totals
- Averages
- Position ranking
- Result approval / locking

So the real order is:
HTML → CSS → JS → app structure → database → backend/Supabase → auth → business rules

Not:
HTML → CSS → JS → Supabase → auth → done

Because this system needs more than just login. It needs data rules, permissions, result calculations, and security. If you do Supabase too early without designing the data model, you will end up rewriting a lot.

Best short version:
- Start with UI structure
- Then the design
- Then interactions
- Then backend/database
- Then auth and permissions
- Then result logic

